#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:10002
msgid "Plot styling"
msgstr "Plot styling"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:10004
msgid ""
"MesPy builds its plots with Matplotlib. Choose a style for the overall look,"
" then adjust individual elements when you need more control."
msgstr ""
"MesPy builds its plots with Matplotlib. Choose a style for the overall look,"
" then adjust individual elements when you need more control."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20003
msgid "Matplotlib styles"
msgstr "Matplotlib styles"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20005
msgid ""
"Both `histogram()` and `lin_fit()` accept a `style` argument. A style sets "
"the overall appearance: fonts, colors, grids, and line properties."
msgstr ""
"Both `histogram()` and `lin_fit()` accept a `style` argument. A style sets "
"the overall appearance: fonts, colors, grids, and line properties."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20007
msgid "You can use:"
msgstr "You can use:"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20009
msgid "Matplotlib styles such as `\"ggplot\"` or `\"dark_background\"`."
msgstr "Matplotlib styles such as `\"ggplot\"` or `\"dark_background\"`."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20010
msgid ""
"MesPy's `\"mespy\"`, `\"report\"`, or `\"column\"` styles, registered when "
"you import MesPy."
msgstr ""
"MesPy's `\"mespy\"`, `\"report\"`, or `\"column\"` styles, registered when "
"you import MesPy."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20011
msgid "A path to a custom `.mplstyle` file, supported by both functions."
msgstr "A path to a custom `.mplstyle` file, supported by both functions."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20012
msgid "`style=None` to keep the currently active Matplotlib configuration."
msgstr "`style=None` to keep the currently active Matplotlib configuration."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20014
msgid ""
"**Both functions currently default to `style=\"mespy\"`.** A named style "
"applies only during the call; it does not change the global configuration."
msgstr ""
"**Both functions currently default to `style=\"mespy\"`.** A named style "
"applies only during the call; it does not change the global configuration."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:20016
msgid ""
"Run the examples in order. These small measurements describe position `y` at"
" time `x`; `sigma_y` gives the position uncertainties."
msgstr ""
"Run the examples in order. These small measurements describe position `y` at"
" time `x`; `sigma_y` gives the position uncertainties."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:50002
msgid ""
"`style=None` is useful after setting a global Matplotlib style. It keeps "
"that configuration while MesPy still supplies its own plot elements and "
"labels."
msgstr ""
"`style=None` is useful after setting a global Matplotlib style. It keeps "
"that configuration while MesPy still supplies its own plot elements and "
"labels."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:50005
msgid "Customizing a histogram"
msgstr "Customizing a histogram"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:50008
msgid "Use MesPy parameters"
msgstr "Use MesPy parameters"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:50010
msgid ""
"Set `bins` to choose the number of bars. Use `xlabel`, `ylabel`, and `title`"
" for the text, and `bar_color` for the bars. Explicit values override the "
"style for that call. `show_mean` and `show_band` control the mean line and "
"the shaded band."
msgstr ""
"Set `bins` to choose the number of bars. Use `xlabel`, `ylabel`, and `title`"
" for the text, and `bar_color` for the bars. Explicit values override the "
"style for that call. `show_mean` and `show_band` control the mean line and "
"the shaded band."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:70003
msgid "Continue editing with Matplotlib"
msgstr "Continue editing with Matplotlib"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:70005
msgid ""
"`histogram()` returns a standard Matplotlib `Figure` and `Axes`. The figure "
"is the whole image; the axes hold the plotted data, labels, and grid."
msgstr ""
"`histogram()` returns a standard Matplotlib `Figure` and `Axes`. The figure "
"is the whole image; the axes hold the plotted data, labels, and grid."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90002
msgid ""
"MesPy creates the statistical visualization; Matplotlib lets you finish it. "
"Save **after** editing to include your changes."
msgstr ""
"MesPy creates the statistical visualization; Matplotlib lets you finish it. "
"Save **after** editing to include your changes."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90004
msgid ""
"The optional `save_path` argument saves during the `histogram()` call. It "
"does not create missing folders, and later edits do not update that saved "
"file automatically."
msgstr ""
"The optional `save_path` argument saves during the `histogram()` call. It "
"does not create missing folders, and later edits do not update that saved "
"file automatically."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90007
msgid "Customizing a linear fit"
msgstr "Customizing a linear fit"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90009
msgid ""
"`lin_fit()` accepts `xlabel`, `ylabel`, and `title` directly. It also has "
"`normalize_residuals` to change how the lower panel displays residuals. "
"Choose fonts, colors, grid settings, figure size, and line widths through "
"`style`."
msgstr ""
"`lin_fit()` accepts `xlabel`, `ylabel`, and `title` directly. It also has "
"`normalize_residuals` to change how the lower panel displays residuals. "
"Choose fonts, colors, grid settings, figure size, and line widths through "
"`style`."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90012
msgid "Use a custom style"
msgstr "Use a custom style"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90014
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For a reusable look, save this file as {download}`styles/lab.mplstyle "
"<styles/lab.mplstyle>` in your project:"
msgstr ""
"For a reusable look, save this file as {download}`styles/lab.mplstyle "
"<styles/lab.mplstyle>` in your project:"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:90020
msgid ""
"The first color is used for the data; the second is used for the fitted line"
" and its band. Pass the file path to `style`:"
msgstr ""
"The first color is used for the data; the second is used for the fitted line"
" and its band. Pass the file path to `style`:"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:110002
msgid ""
"`output` is a base path **without an extension**. MesPy creates the folder "
"if needed and writes `styled_fit.pdf` and `styled_fit.toml`. The PDF is "
"saved while the style is active, including any `savefig.*` settings in that "
"style."
msgstr ""
"`output` is a base path **without an extension**. MesPy creates the folder "
"if needed and writes `styled_fit.pdf` and `styled_fit.toml`. The PDF is "
"saved while the style is active, including any `savefig.*` settings in that "
"style."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:110004
msgid ""
"Choose your style and labels **before calling `lin_fit()`** to include them "
"in the automatic export. Appearance options such as `bar_color` or "
"`title_fontsize` belong to `histogram()`; `lin_fit()` does not accept them."
msgstr ""
"Choose your style and labels **before calling `lin_fit()`** to include them "
"in the automatic export. Appearance options such as `bar_color` or "
"`title_fontsize` belong to `histogram()`; `lin_fit()` does not accept them."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:110007
msgid "Edit the returned figure"
msgstr "Edit the returned figure"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:110009
msgid ""
"`lin_fit()` returns a `LinearFitResult`, rather than a `(fig, ax)` pair. "
"With the default `plot=True`, `result.figure` is a Matplotlib figure you can"
" edit and save. With `plot=False`, it is `None`."
msgstr ""
"`lin_fit()` returns a `LinearFitResult`, rather than a `(fig, ax)` pair. "
"With the default `plot=True`, `result.figure` is a Matplotlib figure you can"
" edit and save. With `plot=False`, it is `None`."

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:110011
msgid "Its two axes are the fit panel followed by the residuals panel:"
msgstr "Its two axes are the fit panel followed by the residuals panel:"

#: ../../source/examples/plot-style.ipynb:130002
msgid ""
"This writes a separate PDF with your edits. It does not update the "
"previously exported `styled_fit.pdf` or the TOML file. The fit results stay "
"the same."
msgstr ""
"This writes a separate PDF with your edits. It does not update the "
"previously exported `styled_fit.pdf` or the TOML file. The fit results stay "
"the same."

